<body>

<div class="header-box">
  <h1>📋 Backend API Feedback & Clarifications</h1>
  <p class="meta-text"><strong>System:</strong> Alpha-10 LMS Back Office · Retail Investor Education</p>
  <p class="meta-text"><strong>Date:</strong> August 22, 2026</p>
  <p class="meta-text"><strong>Target:</strong> Backend Engineering Team</p>
  <p class="meta-text"><strong>Prepared by:</strong> Front-end Team</p>
</div>

<h2>❓ 1. Critical Questions & Clarifications</h2>

<h3>Question A: The <code>code</code> Field & Predefined System Identifiers</h3>
<p>In the education API response, each item contains a <code>code</code> field (e.g., <code>"mutual_funds"</code>):</p>
<pre>{
  "code": "mutual_funds",
  "title": "Mutual Funds"
}</pre>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Question:</strong> Is <code>code</code> an internal identifier expected to match specific mobile app handlers, investment product modules, or asset classes (e.g. <code>mutual_funds</code>, <code>treasury_bills</code>, <code>fixed_deposit</code>, <code>eurobonds</code>)?</li>
  <li><strong>Action / Proposal:</strong> If the backend expects specific predefined codes, please provide a dictionary lookup endpoint:
    <ul>
      <li><code>GET /admin/investment-education/codes</code> (or <code>GET /admin/investment-education/available-types</code>)</li>
      <li>This enables the admin interface to render a clean dropdown selector for admins, eliminating typos or invalid arbitrary slug strings that could break mobile app routing or product linking.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>

<h3>Question B: Domain Flags (<code>capitalGuaranteed</code>, <code>returnsGuaranteed</code>, <code>withdrawalRestrictions</code>)</h3>
<pre>{
  "capitalGuaranteed": false,
  "returnsGuaranteed": false,
  "withdrawalRestrictions": true
}</pre>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Question:</strong> What is the exact business/product logic behind these three boolean flags?</li>
  <li>How are they consumed by the retail mobile app? (e.g., Do they trigger compliance warning banners, regulatory risk disclaimers, or filter rules?)</li>
  <li>Are there validation rules associated with certain <code>code</code> or <code>riskLevel</code> combinations (e.g., if <code>riskLevel == "High"</code>, can <code>capitalGuaranteed</code> ever be <code>true</code>)?</li>
</ul>

<h2>🔴 2. High-Priority API Enhancements</h2>

<h3>1. Implement <code>PATCH</code> Endpoint for Partial Updates (Crucial)</h3>
<p>Currently, only <code>PUT /admin/investment-education/{id}</code> exists, requiring the full object payload even to toggle a status or change display ordering. This is inefficient, prone to race conditions, and heavy for simple admin actions.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Required Action:</div>
  Support <code>PATCH /admin/investment-education/{id}</code> accepting partial updates:
</div>

<p><strong>Example 1: Quick Publish / Unpublish Status Toggle:</strong></p>
<pre>PATCH /admin/investment-education/bbbbbbbb-1111-2222-3333-444444444444
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "isActive": false
}</pre>

<p><strong>Example 2: Display Order Adjustment:</strong></p>
<pre>PATCH /admin/investment-education/bbbbbbbb-1111-2222-3333-444444444444
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "displayOrder": 2
}</pre>

<h3>2. Standardized Pagination Envelope</h3>
<p>The current response returns a raw <code>data: [...]</code> array without pagination metadata. As educational modules grow, paginated queries will be essential.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Required Action:</div>
  Return a paginated metadata envelope and support query parameters <code>?pageNumber=1&amp;pageSize=20</code>:
</div>

<pre>{
  "statusCode": 200,
  "statusMessage": "Success",
  "data": {
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "bbbbbbbb-1111-2222-3333-444444444444",
        "code": "mutual_funds",
        "title": "Mutual Funds",
        "riskLevel": "High",
        "isActive": true,
        "displayOrder": 1
      }
    ],
    "totalCount": 24,
    "pageNumber": 1,
    "pageSize": 20,
    "totalPages": 2
  }
}</pre>

<h3>3. Enum Validation for <code>riskLevel</code></h3>
<ul>
  <li>The sample response returned <code>"riskLevel": "High Risk"</code>, whereas standard contracts use <code>"High"</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Action Required:</strong> Enforce a strict enum contract: <code>["Low", "Medium", "High", "Very High"]</code>.</li>
  <li>Ensure payload validation returns a descriptive <code>400 Bad Request</code> if an unexpected variant is sent.</li>
</ul>

<h3>4. Server-Side Search &amp; Filtering</h3>
<p>Currently, filtering and search are performed on the client.</p>
<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Required Action:</div>
  Support query filtering on <code>GET /admin/investment-education</code>:
</div>
<ul>
  <li><code>?search=mutual</code> (matches code, title, or description)</li>
  <li><code>?riskLevel=High</code></li>
  <li><code>?isActive=true</code></li>
  <li><code>?pageNumber=1&amp;pageSize=20</code></li>
</ul>

<h2>🟡 3. Recommended Content Improvements</h2>

<h3>5. Structured Content Steps for <code>howItWorks</code></h3>
<p>Currently, <code>howItWorksText</code> is a single string with raw newline characters (<code>\n</code>):</p>
<pre>"howItWorksText": "1. Choose a fund that matches your goals.\n2. Deposit an amount..."</pre>
<p>Newline-delimited strings are fragile across mobile clients (iOS/Android) and rich web formatters.</p>
<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Recommended:</div>
  Support structured steps in the JSON schema:
</div>
<pre>"howItWorksSteps": [
  { "step": 1, "title": "Select Fund", "description": "Choose a fund that matches your goals." },
  { "step": 2, "title": "Fund Account", "description": "Deposit an amount at or above the minimum." },
  { "step": 3, "title": "Allocation", "description": "Units are allocated at the prevailing offer price." }
]</pre>

<h3>6. Reading Time &amp; Category (Optional Enhancements)</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>readingTimeMinutes</code>:</strong> Can be estimated on the backend from word count or configured by the admin.</li>
  <li><strong><code>category</code>:</strong> Optional grouping (e.g., <code>Fixed Income</code>, <code>Equities</code>, <code>Savings</code>, <code>Alternative</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>🟢 4. New Endpoints Summary</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th style="width: 40%;">Endpoint</th>
      <th style="width: 15%;">Method</th>
      <th style="width: 45%;">Description</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/admin/investment-education</code></td>
      <td><strong>GET</strong></td>
      <td>List modules with pagination, search, and risk filters</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/admin/investment-education/codes</code></td>
      <td><strong>GET</strong></td>
      <td>Return list of supported/valid system module codes</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>/admin/investment-education/{id}</code></td>
      <td><strong>PATCH</strong></td>
      <td>Partial update (toggle <code>isActive</code>, modify <code>displayOrder</code>)</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<h2>💡 5. Note on Entity ID Handling</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Backend:</strong> <code>id</code> (UUID) in the payload is standard and expected for RESTful CRUD operations (<code>GET /:id</code>, <code>PUT /:id</code>, <code>PATCH /:id</code>, <code>DELETE /:id</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Frontend:</strong> The frontend interface hides all raw database UUIDs from admin view and displays human-friendly identifiers (<code>title</code>, <code>code</code>, <code>order</code>, status badges) instead.</li>
</ul>

</body>
